    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> was established on 29 November 1920 with the Sovietisation of the First Republic, and the treaties of 1921 left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardahan">Ardahan</a> and Surmalu, with Ani and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ararat">Mount Ararat</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhichevan</a> to Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>.</li><li>In its 71 years the republic changed from a largely agricultural hinterland into an industrial centre, and its population grew from about 880,000 in 1926 to 3.3 million in 1989.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh-movement">Karabakh movement</a> of 1988 led to the Declaration of Independence of 23 August 1990, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> became an independent state when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> was dissolved on 26 December 1991.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>According to Khrushchev's memoirs, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> objected to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ararat">Mount Ararat</a> on Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>'s emblem, the Kremlin asked why Turkey had a half moon on its flag, since the moon did not belong to Turkey either; Turkey dropped the issue.</p></aside>
    </section>

