    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> had been part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> since the treaties of Gulistan in 1813 and Turkmenchay in 1828, and Armenian Marxism remained a minority movement until 1917. In May 1918 the First Republic of Armenia emerged, governed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-revolutionary-federation">Armenian Revolutionary Federation</a> and burdened by refugees from the 1915 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> in April 1920, the Armenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> led an uprising at Alexandropol in May, which the government suppressed. In September Turkish forces invaded, and by the end of November the 11th Red Army had entered the republic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a> government sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-khatisian">Alexander Khatisian</a> to negotiate with the Turks and appointed a team under General Drastamat Kanayan to hand power to the Bolsheviks.</p><p>Soviet Armenia was established on 29 November 1920. A pact of 2 December promised the pre-September borders and an amnesty for officials, but the Revkom of Sarkis Kasyan and Avis Nurijanyan, arriving with the Red Army, arrested Dashnak leaders. Its war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> meant, in the words of the Soviet Armenian historian Bagrat Borian, indiscriminate seizures and confiscations; requisitioning and the terror of the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a> led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, under former leaders of the First Republic, to revolt in February 1921 and briefly unseat the government in Yerevan, until the Red Army returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> to suppress the rising.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the early years 80% of the population worked the land. Under Stalin private farms were forcibly collectivised against peasant resistance, especially in Lori, Daralagiaz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zangezur">Zangezur</a>, and industrialisation from 1929 raised industrial output by 1935 to 650% of its 1928 level. The changes broke up the traditional peasant family and village and drove many into the towns; by 1991 about 80% of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> were town dwellers working in heavy industry, management and services. Under Khrushchev farms were divided, private plots allowed, and tobacco, vegetables, grapes and other fruit grown instead of grain alone; under Brezhnev tourism prospered, and the Metsamor Nuclear Power Plant, launched in 1976, supplied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, while pollution and corruption grew.</p><p>The population almost quadrupled, from about 880,000 in 1926 to 3.3 million in 1989, through natural growth and the arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> survivors and their descendants. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> about 150,000 Armenians from countries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> came between 1946 and 1948. They spoke Western Armenian, were received coldly by many, and thousands of them, suspected of Dashnak sympathies, were among those exiled to the Altai Krai in 1949. By the 1979 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> over 99% of the republic's people considered Armenian their national language.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The republic had no forces of its own: its garrison was the Soviet Army's 7th Guards Combined Arms Army of the Transcaucasian Military District, with headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a> and motor rifle divisions at Kirovakan and Leninakan. Its early years were won by force. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> suppressed the revolt of February 1921, and the anti-Soviet rebellion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garegin-nzhdeh">Garegin Nzhdeh</a> in mountainous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zangezur">Zangezur</a> was defeated, its leaders driven across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araks</a> River into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> on 15 July 1921.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> never reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">South Caucasus</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> supplied food, manpower and war material in the Great Patriotic War. An estimated 300,000 to 500,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> served, almost half of whom did not return. Over 60 Armenians became generals, Ivan Isakov an admiral, and Ivan Bagramyan, Hamazasp Babadzhanian and Sergei Khudyakov marshals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>; the aircraft designer Artem Mikoyan co-founded the MiG design bureau. On 7 June 1945 Vyacheslav Molotov told the Turkish ambassador that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardahan">Ardahan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artvin">Artvin</a> and Surmalu should be returned to the Soviet Union in the name of Armenia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, a claim backed by the Catholicos and every part of the diaspora, the Dashnaksutiun included. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truman-doctrine">Truman Doctrine</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> turned to the West, and the Soviet Union dropped the claim.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The republic was governed by the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party of the Soviet Union</a>: the First Secretary was its administrative head, the Chairman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-european-union">Council of Ministers</a> headed the government, and the Supreme Soviet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>, elected for five years, was its legislature. From 1930 it was divided into up to 37 raions. In 1921 Lenin sent Alexander Miasnikian to replace the Revkom's methods with a moderate policy attuned to national feeling, and under korenizatsiya Armenian became the official language, illiterate citizens up to the age of 50 were sent to school, and the spelling was reformed.</p><p>Under Stalin the republic's leadership was destroyed. Beria's struggle with First Secretary Aghasi Khanjian ended with Khanjian's killing on 9 July 1936; Beria's men, Amatuni Amatuni and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> chief Khachik Mughdusi, arrested 1,365 people in ten months, among them the writers Charents, Bakunts and Zabel Yesayan. After Sahak Ter-Gabrielyan's death in NKVD custody in August 1937, Stalin sent Georgy Malenkov and Anastas Mikoyan to purge the party; more than a thousand were arrested and Grigory Arutinov became party leader. Catholicos Khoren I was murdered in 1938 and the Catholicate of Ejmiatsin closed. In 1978 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> defeated a proposal to remove their languages' official status.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The NEP years brought a cultural revival under the formula 'national in form, socialist in content'. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-tamanian">Alexander Tamanian</a> began his city plan for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>, an institute for culture and history opened at Ejmiatsin in 1921, the Yerevan Opera Theatre and a dramatic theatre were built, and Martiros Saryan, Yeghishe Charents, Avetik Isahakyan and others produced popular works of art and literature. Hamo Beknazarian directed the first Armenian feature film, Namus, in 1925, the first Kurdish film, Zare, in 1926, and the first Armenian sound film, Pepo, in 1935; the Kurdish newspaper Ria Taza was founded in 1930.</p><p>After Stalin's death Mikoyan urged the rehabilitation of Charents and Miasnikian, purge victims were rehabilitated, and the Stalin statue over Yerevan was pulled down in 1962 and replaced by Mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in 1967. The Matenadaran opened in 1959 to house the nationalised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of Ejmiatsin, the Arpa–Sevan tunnel was built to save <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-sevan">Lake Sevan</a>, and contacts with the diaspora revived. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> memorial at Tsitsernakaberd followed in 1967 and Rafayel Israyelian's memorial to the Battle of Sardarabad in 1968; Karen Demirchyan's government built the Yerevan Metro and Zvartnots International Airport. Soviet textbooks called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, and Armenia in particular, the oldest civilisation on Soviet territory.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The question of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a>, given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> in 1921, never went away: its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> appealed to Khrushchev in 1962, Anton Kochinyan asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in vain for unification in 1966, and the petitions went on through the 1970s. Under Gorbachev's glasnost and perestroika the Supreme Soviet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh-autonomous-oblast">Nagorno-Karabakh Autonomous Oblast</a> voted on 20 February 1988 to unite with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Demonstrations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>'s Theater Square in support grew into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh-movement">Karabakh movement</a>. Violence against Armenians in Sumgait, ethnic rioting, the devastating earthquake of 1988, in which the shoddy apartment blocks of the Brezhnev years were the first to collapse, and a further pogrom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> turned Armenians against the Kremlin.</p><p>Moscow's indecision on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a>, the slow relief after the earthquake and the failing economy widened the gap between Yerevan and the centre. On 23 August 1990 the Supreme Soviet adopted the Declaration of Independence, naming the state the Republic of Armenia. Armenia boycotted the union-wide referendum of 17 March 1991 on preserving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and after the failed coup in Moscow it confirmed its independence by referendum on 21 September 1991, with more than 99% in favour. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belovezha-accords">Belovezha Accords</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union">dissolution of the Soviet Union</a> on 26 December 1991 the Armenian SSR ceased to exist; its constitution of 1978 remained in force until July 1995.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Armenian SSR was created in 1920 when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> took over the First Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Through collectivisation, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and the return of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> survivors it became an industrial republic of 3.3 million, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh-movement">Karabakh movement</a> of 1988 led to its independence in 1991.</em></p>

